 r"I am writing to apply for the Data Scientist position in the Investment Bank AI Delivery Hub at UBS in London. I completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London in September 2026, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have built AI and machine learning solutions with the people who will use them, and measured whether they actually work before recommending them.",

 r"What attracts me most to this role is that the work has a visible end point: AI capabilities that business, risk and technology teams across the Investment Bank use to save time and reduce risk, rather than prototypes that stay in a notebook. The posting describes contributing across the whole lifecycle, from discovery and prototyping through to deployment and adoption, and applying model evaluation to demonstrate impact. That is the part of applied AI I enjoy most. I like taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern on a patient-facing medical AI product for a pharmaceutical client. I built a Python pipeline that called six LLM APIs with retrieval and search augmentation and scored more than 2,400 responses with an LLM-as-judge framework. With the client's clinical experts I designed a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension responsible-AI rubric with red-line rules, which the client adopted as its acceptance standard, and I ran blinded A/B tests to measure whether a medically reviewed knowledge graph improved the answers. I also built a regex and LLM extraction pipeline that turned more than 4,800 unstructured patient threads into structured data. The lesson was that an AI solution is only ready for a regulated setting once you have a reliable way to tell when it is wrong.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I analyse large merchant and transaction datasets in SQL and Python and build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations, product and commercial teams use. When I was asked to complete a repetitive campaign configuration by hand, I worked through a few examples, saw the underlying logic and used Claude Code to build a multi-step agent with a validation step to check its own output, which cut the time the task took by around 40 to 50 percent. Persuading a sceptical colleague that it was accurate was harder than building it, and it taught me that adoption depends on trust as much as on the model.",

 r"My MSc dissertation compared logistic regression with tree ensembles on 458,840 UK Biobank participants, with careful attention to calibration, and used doubly robust causal forests with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding. During my MSc I also led a group project that fine-tuned PubMedBERT-style models on radiology reports and evaluated them against simpler baselines. Both taught me to choose a model by evidence rather than by fashion, and to report a weak result honestly.",

 r"I would bring UBS hands-on experience with LLMs, RAG and agentic workflows, rigorous model evaluation and responsible-AI practice, strong Python and statistics, and the habit of explaining technical results clearly to business stakeholders. I have the right to work in the UK until January 2029 without sponsorship. I am based in London and happy to work from the London office.",

 r"Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience in applied AI and model evaluation could contribute to the IB AI Delivery Hub.",
